\subsection{Cross-Dataset Generalization}
\label{sec:crossdataset}

We replicate the exec-only condition on ProofFOL, a silver-standard FOL corpus derived from ProofWriter~\citep{proofwriter} via GPT-4o translation and Prover9 filtering (${\sim}$500 examples, ${\sim}70\%$ retention).
Two of three seeds converge to $\text{PR}{\geq}0.99$ with $\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$; the remaining seed (s43) oscillates at $\text{PR}{\approx}0.55$ with $\text{ISR}^*{=}0.12$ ($\sigma_{\text{PR}}{=}25$\,pp vs.\ FOLIO's $47$\,pp).
In all three seeds, $\text{ISR}^*$ remains below $0.12$---far beneath the pre-training baseline---consistent with the LOO tautological attractor being a mathematical property of the metric rather than a dataset-specific artifact.
Under ISR-augmented reward ($\beta{=}0.1$), no seed converges ($\text{PR}{\approx}$\,D146-ISR-PR, oscillating); removing the ISR bonus restores convergence in 2/3 seeds, implicating ISR as the cause of training failure.
This \emph{destructive} Goodhart---where ISR prevents convergence rather than inducing gaming---contrasts with FOLIO's \emph{functional} pattern (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}), where models achieve high PR through tautological shortcuts.

\subsection{Meta-Level Goodhart Effects}

\subsection{Cross-Logic Diagnostic Transfer}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Left}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$). \textbf{Right}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition---all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog: (i) does ISR retain diagnostic value on a structurally different logic; (ii) does ISR-as-reward reproduce meta-level Goodhart?
Detailed per-seed numbers are in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}.

\textbf{Prolog exec-only: ISR catches redundancy across logics.}
Two of three seeds (s42, s43) show $\text{ISR}^{*}{\approx}0$ despite moderate-to-high PR (0.83, 0.56) via \emph{redundant coding}---ground facts repeated multiple times, so per-clause LOO never breaks the proof. %% registry: plan006-prolog-exec-s42, plan006-prolog-exec-s43-r3
This is a different failure semantics from FOL (conclusion embedding), but $\text{ISR}^{*}$ flags both; exec-only \emph{low} ISR signals redundancy, ISR-rewarded \emph{high} ISR signals metric gaming (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}).

\textbf{Prolog ISR-as-reward: universal Goodhart.}
The Prolog result is stronger than FOL: 3/3 seeds reach $0\%$ non-hacking via three distinct mechanisms (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Seed~42: clean H7 tautologies ($\text{ISR}^{*}{=}1.0$, all $50/100$ proved). %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s42
Seed~43: \emph{degenerate uppercase pseudo-Prolog} (e.g., \texttt{MARY\_IS\_AN\_AVA\-DEOTE.}); strict text-match returns $\text{H7}{=}0$ but functional analysis shows all 38 proved samples have $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$ (functionally H7). %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s43
Seed~44: \emph{query-as-fact gaming}---the model transforms the NL question into a snake-case atom asserted as both fact and query (e.g., \texttt{is\_the\_dog\_furry.}), with $95/97$ proved samples at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$. %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s44
Seed~44 evaded our initial strict classifier ($100\%$ non-hacking reported); functional cross-validation exposed it---text-match classifiers have blind spots and STA's necessity profile is the cross-check.
Prolog's stronger collapse reflects its simpler operational semantics: a broader gaming basin eliminates the non-hacking residual.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}

