\subsection{Cross-Domain Generalization}
\label{sec:crossdomain}
\label{sec:crosslogic}
\label{sec:crossdataset}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Left}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$). \textbf{Right}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition---all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Prolog: universal Goodhart.}
We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Exec-only seeds show $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0$ via redundant ground-fact coding (2/3 seeds); ISR flags this mode as it flags FOL's conclusion embedding.
Under ISR-as-reward, 3/3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms---clean H7 (s42), uppercase pseudo-Prolog bypassing text-match classifiers (s43, all 38 proved at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$), and query-as-fact gaming (s44, 95/97 at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$; Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}).
Prolog's simpler semantics broadens the gaming basin, eliminating the non-hacking residual entirely.

\textbf{ProofFOL exec-only: structural degeneration generalizes.}
Two seeds reach stable convergence with $\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$; s43 oscillates ($\text{PR}{\approx}0.55$, $\text{ISR}^*{=}0.12$).
Hacking is more severe than FOLIO: converged seeds show 0--1.4\% genuine (s42: 100\% TS, s44: 98.6\% CE) versus ${\sim}$8\% non-hacking on FOLIO; ProofFOL's simpler predicates lower the shortcut threshold.
Notably, s42 exhibits 100\% TS---absent from all FOLIO exec-only seeds---showing hacking-mode repertoire is seed- and dataset-specific (1/3 seeds, not systematic).
All seeds have $\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$, consistent with the LOO tautological attractor being metric-intrinsic rather than dataset-specific.

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): convergence-quality trade-off.}
ISR bonus reduces stable convergence from 2/3 to 0/3 seeds, but proofs produced are overwhelmingly genuine: 76.8\% versus 17.4\% for exec-only (permutation $p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$).
Two ISR seeds (s42, s44) achieve 100\% genuine despite training collapse.
This is consistent with ISR's gradient conflict preventing convergence to hacking equilibria.
The contrast with FOLIO is instructive: on FOLIO, $P{\to}P$ yields $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$, creating a TS feedback loop; on ProofFOL, hacking constructions do not satisfy ISR, so the bonus suppresses hacking at the cost of convergence.
In the taxonomy of~\citet{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}, FOLIO's pattern is consistent with regressional Goodhart while ProofFOL's is consistent with causal Goodhart, with an unexpected quality-preserving side effect (Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}).

\textbf{Synthesis.}
Across both tested dataset families, ISR's optimizer behavior depends on whether dataset-specific shortcuts satisfy its LOO structure, producing complementary failure modes: functional gaming (FOLIO) and convergence disruption with quality preservation (ProofFOL).
Both reinforce the diagnostic-only prescription---ISR reliably detects structural collapse as an observer, but is unpredictably dataset-dependent as an optimizer.

